<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE COLOUR BEHIND THE FIRST FRAME.

  The launch window used to be @android:color/white with the logo commented
  out — the Flutter template's untouched default. So opening the app showed a
  bare white rectangle with nothing on it, then the real splash: a white flash
  on every launch, and on a phone in dark mode a bright one.

  These are the app's own surface colours, so the window that appears before
  Flutter has drawn anything is already the app's background rather than a
  stranger's.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="lipSurface">#F6F8FC</color>

    <!--
      THE COLOUR BEHIND THE LAUNCHER ICON, and it must never leave this file.

      mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml draws the adaptive icon's background
      from it. Adding lipSurface here REPLACED it rather than joining it, and
      that broke `flutter build appbundle` outright:

        error: resource color/ic_launcher_background not found
        error: failed linking file resources

      Nothing caught it. flutter analyze does not read Android resources, no
      test touches them, and this is the only build in the project that links
      them at all — so the app compiled, analysed and tested perfectly while
      being impossible to ship. The two release runs after that change died on
      Actions billing before Gradle started, which hid it for a second time.

      It is deliberately NOT given a values-night variant. A launcher icon
      does not follow the phone's theme; a home screen with two different
      LockInPoint marks depending on the hour is not a dark mode feature.
    -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#0F2C86</color>
</resources>
